      "author": "Hollowmere team",
      "story": "Tamsin Timber's Camp 4 was waiting on the road crew with fourteen men and a held payroll when the sickness came up the logging road. Walt Brody, the foreman, put the six who ran fevers in the bunkhouse, barricaded its front, had the drying-room door padlocked and kept the key in his office desk; then he moved in with them to keep watch, and took the payroll with him to the safe in his room. Dee Pruitt kept cooking. She left trays at the drying-room door until nobody took them, hung the office keys on the pantry nail like Walt asked and wrote it all on her order pad. The rest of the crew walked out toward Pell's Crossing. Walt's last note is still on his desk: pay the boys.",
